# One-page situation report template (English).
# Placeholders are filled from the results store ONLY (no LLM, no invented
# numbers). Rendered by reporting/sitrep.py; every number in the output is
# checked by reporting/validator.py.

# FloodSight situation report
## {region_label} - flood date {flood_date}

**Educational prototype - outputs are estimates, not confirmed damage.**

## Summary
Analysis of Sentinel-1 radar pair (same orbit track {orbit}) and pre-event
OpenStreetMap (snapshot {osm_snapshot_date}) over the area
{bbox_label}. New standing flood water detected on the post-event date:
{flood_area_km2} km2. Probable debris / surface change:
{debris_area_km2} km2. The maps show the state on the satellite dates, not
the flood peak.

## Key figures
- Buildings likely hit: {buildings_likely} (possibly: {buildings_possible})
  of {buildings_total} mapped in the area
- Roads inside the flood/debris extent: {roads_km} km
- Bridges affected: {bridges_affected} of {bridges_total}
- Settlements likely cut off: {cutoff_likely}; possibly cut: {cutoff_possible};
  still connected: {cutoff_connected}
- Settlements already isolated before the event (not flood-related):
  {cutoff_already}

## Critical infrastructure
Top affected bridges: {bridges_list}

## Cut-off communities
{cutoff_names}

## Data sources and dates
- Sentinel-1 pre-event: {s1_pre} ({s1_pre_date})
- Sentinel-1 post-event: {s1_post} ({s1_post_date})
- OSM snapshot: {osm_snapshot_date} (pre-event; rule-enforced)
- DEM: Copernicus GLO-30
- Detection method: {method}

## Limitations
Steep Himalayan terrain causes radar shadow and layover; ~{invalid_share}
of pixels are unreliable there. OSM coverage in remote areas is incomplete,
so cut-off results are a lower bound. Flood water may have receded before
the first usable post-event scene. Nepali output should be reviewed by a
native speaker before operational use.

